
<p>Mary Alyce Stephens (née Neader), posting on Cringely’s Facebook account:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>Hello, this is Mary Alyce, Bob’s wife. I am coming to you with
immense sadness to let you know that Bob passed away. His obituary
follows. His family is not requesting anything, however, if you
would like to make a donation in his honor, please consider <a href="https://www.heart.org/">The
American Heart Association</a> or <a href="https://sids.org/">The American SIDS Institute</a>. [...]</p>

<p>Writing as Robert X. Cringely, Mark became a distinctive voice in
technology journalism. He had an unusual ability to understand not
only technology but also the human beings behind it — the
dreamers, inventors, entrepreneurs, eccentrics, and risk-takers
who were changing the world. He could take a complicated subject
and make it understandable, entertaining, funny, and deeply human.
He was skeptical when skepticism was warranted, irreverent when
others were overly serious, and never particularly interested in
following conventional wisdom. That independence was not simply
part of his writing. It was Mark.</p>

<p>He followed the beat of his own drummer throughout his life. He
was brilliant, funny, opinionated, curious, generous,
exasperating, and unforgettable — sometimes all within the same
conversation. He loved a good story and knew how to tell one. He
could make you laugh, make you think, make you argue with him, and
occasionally drive you crazy. Life with Mark was rarely dull.</p>

<p>Mark’s life also included heartbreaking loss. He was preceded in
death by his beloved sons, Chase and Cole Stephens, as well as his
parents, Ray and Lois Stephens and father-in-law Jim Neader. I
could also add brother-in-law Jimmy Neader and niece Ericka
Neader, but they were a thorn in our side.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>That last dig, at her own dead brother and niece, offers a hint at why Cringely loved her. He wasn’t one to pull punches either.</p>

<p>Her full obituary for Cringely/Stephens is visible without being logged into Facebook (I’ve still never created one), but <a href="https://x.com/stevesi/status/2109528428265570793">Steven Sinofsky kindly posted it</a> to his account on X. Pick your poison. <strong>Update:</strong> <a href="https://www.hillandwood.com/obituaries/Mark-Stephens?obId=49806651">Here’s a version hosted by the funeral home</a>.</p>

